Spirit of the liscense
In the history of computing - there have been many other Operating Systems -
Almost universally, an Operating System and the software built around it - 
Will conform to atleast "A" set of standards.

This is termed heritage, or lineage, a common ancestry, "Genetic unix"

These operating systems share actual source code, descending from and tracing back to the original AT&T Bell Labs Unix.

The development of Unix wasn't driven by corporate milestones, market research, or profit margins. 

Instead - It was driven by peer respect and the joy of creation.
In a culture of continuous, playful iteration.


The Spirit of Intellectual Generosity

Because Bell Labs couldn't sell Unix as a product - they did something that sparked a global revolution: they gave it away.
They shipped tapes containing the Unix source code to universities for the mere cost of shipping and media.
This triggered a wave of wonder that rippled out of Murray Hill and across the globe.
Students and professors at Berkeley, MIT, and beyond didn't just use Unix; they could look under the hood, see Ritchie and Thompson’s elegant code, and contribute their own ideas.
The excitement of the Bell Labs team became contagious, birthing the modern hacker culture and laying the philosophical foundations for the open-source movement.
Ultimately, the wonder of developing Unix was the feeling of catching lightning in a bottle.
A small band of friends, operating in the margins of a corporate giant, rewrote the rules of human-computer interaction simply because they wanted a better way to talk to machines and to each other.

The nature of unix-like and posix software "is" - that shared heritage.
That - is what this liscense is meant to protect.

That shared heritage, is the foundational basis of modern computing.
We wouldn't have what we do today, if that never happened.

